-- **Reaching for `numbered` on a slide with no heading, or expecting it on a `title` / `closing`.** The mark rides the heading's `::after` — deliberately, because `section::after` is the engine's page number and `silent` / `no-pagination` null it. No heading, no numeral — and the counter still advances, so the next divider reads one higher and the series silently skips. The heading is a required slot here anyway. `numbered` is a divider modifier only: a bookend is not a section, so `title` and `closing` do not take it. Like every divider it keeps the deck's header and footer, so a `_footer:` caption reads there too. The mark's band is reserved, so a long heading can no longer climb into the numeral — it pins under the band with a constant 2.5cqi of clearance and grows downward instead, and far enough down the slide genuinely overflows and the export says so. `light` and `qr` need the band too: measured without it they collide at -15.2 and -85.3.
+- **Reaching for `numbered` on a slide with no heading, or expecting it on a `title` / `closing`.** The mark rides the heading's `::after` — deliberately, because `section::after` is the engine's page number and `silent` / `no-pagination` null it. No heading, no numeral — and the counter still advances, so the next divider reads one higher and the series silently skips. The heading is a required slot here anyway. `numbered` is a divider modifier only: a bookend is not a section, so `title` and `closing` do not take it. Like every divider it keeps the deck's header and footer, so a `_footer:` caption reads there too. The mark's band is reserved, so a long heading can no longer climb into the numeral — it pins under the band with a constant 2.5cqi of clearance and grows downward instead, and far enough down the slide genuinely overflows and the export says so. `subsection` and `qr` need the band too: measured without it they collide at -15.2 and -85.3.
 
 ## When to use
 
 - **Major section starts.** Marks the boundary between two themed sections of the deck. The dark canvas is a strong context-switch signal — use it when the audience needs to re-orient.
 - **Sparingly.** A 30-slide deck typically has 3-5 dividers. More becomes navigation noise; the signal weakens if every third slide is a divider.
 - **With an eyebrow.** An inline-code paragraph above the heading stamps a section number or category label. Useful for serialized decks where the audience needs to remember which section they're in.
-- **Light variant for sub-section orientation.** The `light` variant keeps the bright body-slide canvas and centers the heading at h2 weight — a lighter context switch for narrowing focus within a section, between a dark divider and the content slides. (Absorbed the standalone `subtopic` component on 2026-06-07.)
+- **Light variant for sub-section orientation.** The `subsection` variant centers the heading at h2 weight with no rail — a lighter context switch for narrowing focus within a section, between a divider and the content slides. It takes every divider's field, so it follows the color mode. (Absorbed the standalone `subtopic` component on 2026-06-07; named `divider light` until 2026-11-23.)
 
 ## When NOT to use
 
 - **More than five per deck.** Each divider is a hard context switch. Too many dilutes the signal and slows the audience. Group related content under fewer sections instead.
-- **Section title that doesn't earn a section.** If the next few slides aren't one unit, use the `light` variant. Keep the dark divider for real section starts.
+- **Section title that doesn't earn a section.** If the next few slides aren't one unit, use the `subsection` variant. Keep the default divider for real section starts.
 - **Header or footer overrides.** Don't blank them with `_header: ''` or `_footer: ''`. A divider keeps the deck's header and footer; drop one with `no-header` / `no-footer`, or all with `silent`.
 
